{
  "result": "There is a small hour before sunrise when the town belongs to nobody and the streets feel borrowed from a dream. I like to walk then with no destination in mind, letting my feet choose corners while my thoughts loosen and drift. The bakery on Alder Street is always first awake, and its windows glow amber against the blue quiet of morning. Inside, an old radio murmurs weather reports to rows of cooling bread, and the baker hums without ever quite finding the melody. Sometimes he sees me passing and taps the glass, offering yesterday muffins through the side door with solemn generosity. I accept because refusal would break some unspoken ritual, and because warm cinnamon can persuade almost anyone to believe in simple mornings. Further along, the river repeats its patient argument with the stones, never winning, never tiring, always arriving somewhere else. A heron stands there like a punctuation mark, deciding whether the sentence of the day deserves continuation or elegant pause. I envy that stillness, though I suspect stillness practiced so perfectly must contain a hundred hidden adjustments. By seven, bicycles begin stitching the avenues together, bells ring, shutters clatter, and the borrowed dream politely returns its keys. People emerge carrying lists, deadlines, umbrellas, apologies, and bright intentions that will soften by noon into manageable compromises. Yet for a while everything seems possible, as if the city has forgiven itself overnight and agreed to begin again gently. I carry that mercy home and keep it quietly lit.",
